history.notes:
## Changes
- **Road noise maps (CRTN) now calculate about twice as fast.** On P2000 (Layout Sketch, Day and Night, 4 m grid, 5,307 nodes, 294 buildings), a road contour map now takes about 50 s instead of about 100 s. On scenario S1 it takes about 40 s instead of about 85 s. The CPU time per map node has fallen from about 171 ms to about 81 ms.
  - **The per-segment calculation is now compiled.** For each map node, AURIS splits every road into segments wherever screening, buildings or ground cover change (CRTN paragraphs 11 and 22), then calculates each segment's corrections. That work now runs as compiled native code, from the source and receiver geometry to the final segment level. It covers:
    - Charts 7 to 12;
    - double screening (paragraph 35);
    - building sections (paragraph 24);
    - terrain crests on the propagation profile;
    - reflections from facades across the road (paragraph 26.2).
  - **Projects with or without surveyed terrain both benefit.** Projects that use AURIS's flat datum instead of a terrain survey also take the faster route.
  - **The first map after installing is fast too.** The compiled code is prepared when the app is built, so the first map after an install or update does not compile it.

  **Calculated levels are unchanged.** Every node of both periods on two P2000 scenarios is identical to 0.72 bit for bit. So is every CRTN grid across the project corpus, from surveyed-terrain and flat-datum projects alike, and the reference results are unchanged. The compiled code reproduces Python's own arithmetic exactly, including the last binary digit of its square roots, distances, powers and trigonometry. The calculation identity changes only because the code that produces the results changed.
